# PortLess — production readiness to-do

Everything that has to be true before PortLess moves real buyer and supplier money, plus the
features that would raise profit per deal. Written 2026-11-13 from the code on `main`
(backend `df50ecc`, UI `f229046`), the production readiness gate, the test catalogue and the
end-to-end automation runs.

**Priority** — **P0**: blocks the first real trade (money, law or data can be lost or wrong).
**P1**: needed before running more than a handful of deals a month. **P2**: improvement.
**Owner** — (Eng) engineering · (Ops) operations / trade desk · (Legal) lawyer or chartered
accountant · (Biz) founder / business · (QA) quality.

---

**Layout** — grouped by priority first (P0, then P1, then P2), then by area. Area numbers (1.1, 3.3, 5.5 …) are kept in every priority so a reference such as "see 3.3" points to the same area wherever its items sit. Background for an area is in the appendix at the end.

---

## Where things stand today

- [ ] **P0 (Eng/Ops)** Production readiness gate (`ENV_PROFILE=production`, `GET /health/readiness`): **12 fail, 5 warn, 19 ok**. Production refuses to boot until every fail is fixed — section 1 lists each one.
- Backend tests: 4574 passed, 1 skipped. Coverage 95.46 % line + branch (measured 2026-11-09, before the latest features — re-measure).
- End-to-end automation: TC-E2E-000 / UC-15 (scan opportunity → PAID on every simulator) passes 56/56 checks; UC-01 passes 35/35. UC-02 … UC-14 were not re-run after the latest changes.
- Gherkin catalogue: 589 cases. **81 are browser-only and nothing drives the console. 34 backend cases still have unautomated steps — 24 of them P0.** 95 production-risk cases (TC-RSK, 52 P0) were added on 2026-11-13 and none is automated yet; 32 new browser cases (TC-WEB) are in `docs/testing/BROWSER_SCENARIOS.md` (section 6).
- UI repo: type-check passes; **no tests of any kind, no CI**.
- Neither repo has CI. Nothing runs the test suites automatically on a push to `main`.

---

## Go-live blockers — the short list

Everything here is P0; the areas are named in brackets.

- [ ] Real credentials for every provider in the money path, each verified against the provider's own sandbox first (section 1, section 2).
- [ ] Legal set-up of the exporter, the escrow route and the supplier payment route signed off by a lawyer / CA (section 3).
- [ ] Decide how Indian suppliers are paid (INR in India vs Payoneer USD) — today's design may not be legal for a domestic purchase (section 3.3).
- [ ] Money writes made atomic and reconciled daily against the provider statements (section 4).
- [ ] PI acceptance that names a different price must not pass as an acceptance (section 5.1).
- [ ] Local suppliers can be added by hand and start the workflow (section 5.5).
- [ ] Durable event bus, backups, monitoring, alerting and single-instance background watchers (section 7).
- [ ] CI on both repos; the 24 P0 catalogue cases automated; UC-01 … UC-15 green on a nightly run (section 6).
- [ ] One pilot trade with a small, real lot, a known buyer and a known supplier, before opening the funnel (section 6.7).
- [ ] Test strategy in place: unit, integration, sanity / smoke and regression suites defined, in CI and green (6.9 – 6.13).

---

## P0 — must be done before the first real trade

81 items.

### 1.1 Money — Accounts, credentials and configuration (readiness gate)
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** `escrow_provider` FAIL — open Tazapay and Escrow.com business accounts; set `TAZAPAY_API_KEY` / `_API_SECRET` / `_WEBHOOK_SECRET` / `_ENV` and `ESCROW_COM_API_KEY` / `_EMAIL` / `_WEBHOOK_SECRET`. Drop a provider from `ESCROW_PROVIDERS_ENABLED` until its account is live.
- [ ] **(Eng)** `money_webhook_secrets` FAIL — set every money webhook secret (`TAZAPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET`, `ESCROW_COM_WEBHOOK_SECRET`, `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`, `PAYONEER_WEBHOOK_SECRET`); today escrow-funded and payout webhooks are accepted unsigned.
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** `payout_rail` FAIL — `PAYONEER_CLIENT_ID` / `_CLIENT_SECRET` unset, supplier payouts are stubs. Do not set this until section 3.3 is decided.
- [ ] **(Biz)** Set `FOUR_EYES_THRESHOLD_USD` (`.env.example` suggests 25000). Unset means one person can release any amount.
- [ ] **(Biz)** Keep `AUTONOMY_MONEY_CEILING=L1` (or L0) and no `AUTONOMY_MONEY_AUTO_APPROVE_BELOW_USD` for the first months (readiness shows both ok today — keep them that way).

### 1.2 Inspection, freight, customs — Accounts, credentials and configuration (readiness gate)
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** `qc_provider` FAIL — QIMA account: `QIMA_API_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `QIMA_ACCOUNT`, `QIMA_PASSWORD`, plus `QIMA_PRODUCT_CATEGORY_ID` / `_FAMILY_ID` / `_TYPE_ID` from `GET /parameter/v2/product-tree` for each product line.
- [ ] **(Biz)** `qc_peo_service_type` WARN — QIMA documents no booking id for the pre-customs inspection (PEO). Ask QIMA for the id on your account and set `QIMA_SERVICE_TYPE_PEO`, or a buyer's PEO request is refused at booking.
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** `freight_provider` FAIL — Maersk account: `MAERSK_CONSUMER_KEY`, `MAERSK_CLIENT_SECRET`, `MAERSK_CUSTOMER_CODE`, `MAERSK_SERVICE_CONTRACT`, `MAERSK_WEBHOOK_SECRET`, and **`MAERSK_CARRIER_SERVICE_CODE` (the Maersk service on your lane — booking fails without it)**.
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** `customs` FAIL — `ICEGATE_API_KEY` unset, the Shipping Bill / LEO filing is a stub. Either get ICEGATE access or decide that the customs broker (CHA) files manually and record that in the flow.

### 1.3 E-mail — Accounts, credentials and configuration (readiness gate)
- [ ] **(Eng/Ops)** `smtp` FAIL — outbound mailbox (`GMAIL_SENDER_ADDRESS` / `GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD` or a transactional provider). Today every PI, PO and negotiation e-mail is log-only.
- [ ] **(Eng/Ops)** `imap` FAIL — inbound polling (`IMAP_USER` / `IMAP_PASSWORD`), or SendGrid / Mailgun inbound with `SENDGRID_WEBHOOK_PUBLIC_KEY` / `MAILGUN_SIGNING_KEY`. Without it every reply is pasted in by hand.

### 1.4 Documents and identity — Accounts, credentials and configuration (readiness gate)
- [ ] **(Eng)** `document_store` FAIL — Google Drive (`GOOGLE_DRIVE_CLIENT_ID` / `_CLIENT_SECRET` / `_REFRESH_TOKEN` via `scripts/google_drive_authorize.py`). Today documents land on local disk and are lost with the container.
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** `exporter_identity` FAIL — `DEALMAKER_PORTLESS_ENTITY_IEC` / `_GSTIN`; the PI and PO carry a placeholder exporter.
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** `documents_exporter` FAIL — `PORTLESS_ADDRESS`, `PORTLESS_IEC`, `PORTLESS_GSTIN`, `PORTLESS_LUT_ARN`, `PORTLESS_AD_CODE`; without them the CI / PL / SDF / Shipping Bill checklist are issued as DRAFT.
- [ ] **(Legal)** `documents_contract_law` WARN — `PORTLESS_CONTRACT_LAW` (governing law + arbitration seat); the sales contract is DRAFT without it.

### 1.5 Platform — Accounts, credentials and configuration (readiness gate)
- [ ] **(Eng)** `event_bus` FAIL — NATS JetStream (Synadia `.creds` file or `NATS_CREDS_FILE`). With the in-process bus an event published during a crash is lost.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Sign-in: `PORTLESS_ACCOUNT_ID` from auth-service (still awaited), `AUTH_GATEWAY_URL`, `AUTH_PORTLESS_EMAILS` (staff allowlist), `FRONTEND_URL`. Keep `AUTH_TRUST_GATEWAY_HEADERS=false` unless only the gateway can reach the backend.

### 2.1 Tazapay (escrow) — Provider contracts not yet confirmed
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** Tazapay v3 documents no release endpoint and no escrow webhook event names. PortLess books a stage as released when an uploaded document shows `verified` or a webhook type in `TAZAPAY_RELEASE_EVENTS` arrives. Confirm with Tazapay: the event names, whether one escrow can pay out in four stages (30/40/20/10), and who triggers each payout.
- [ ] **(Eng/QA)** Run the full escrow life cycle on Tazapay's **own sandbox** (`TAZAPAY_ENV=sandbox`): create, buyer pays, partial pay, document per stage, payout, refund, expire, webhook signature.

### 2.3 Maersk (carrier) — Provider contracts not yet confirmed
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** The Maersk contract was built from mirrored OpenAPI files (DCSA-aligned), not from Maersk's portal. Check on your Maersk account: **(1) the token request fields, (2) the webhook payload and signature, (3) shipping instructions** — these are not in the mirrored APIs, so they are submitted on the Maersk portal for now and PortLess picks up the draft B/L.

### 2.4 QIMA (inspection) — Provider contracts not yet confirmed
- [ ] **(Biz)** PEO service type id (see 1.2).
- [ ] **(Eng/QA)** Run book → validate → inspect → report → PDF → cancel on QIMA's real pre-production API (`ppapi.qima.com`) with your account; the simulator is built from the public docs.

### 2.5 Payoneer, ICEGATE, Stripe — Provider contracts not yet confirmed
- [ ] **(Legal/Biz)** Payoneer — see 3.3 before integrating.

### 2.6 Data providers — Provider contracts not yet confirmed
- [ ] **(Legal/Eng)** **DGFT denied-entity check is a stub** (`DgftConnector.book` always answers "not denied"). Load a real copy of the DGFT denied list (it is published as a document, not an API) and refresh it on a schedule.

### 3.1 Exporter registration — Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)
- [ ] **(Legal)** IEC, GSTIN, GST LUT (letter of undertaking for zero-rated exports), AD bank code registered with customs, and the bank's EDPMS set-up.
- [ ] **(Legal)** Export promotion registration for the commodities traded (e.g. APEDA RCMC for agricultural products). **Basmati rice (HS 10063020, the product used in testing) export contracts must be registered with APEDA before shipment — confirm and add the step to the flow.**
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Commodity-specific export policy changes often (bans, minimum export prices, duties). Confirm every HS code against current DGFT notifications and record it with `PUT /deals/compliance/hs-policy/{hs}`; set an owner to review DGFT notifications weekly.

### 3.2 Money route and foreign exchange — Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)
- [ ] **(Legal)** Receiving export proceeds through Tazapay / Escrow.com: confirm the route complies with FEMA and RBI rules for cross-border payment aggregators, that proceeds reach PortLess's AD bank in time, and that an e-BRC / FIRC can be obtained for each shipment (needed for GST refunds and EDPMS closure).
- [ ] **(Legal)** Export proceeds must be realised within the RBI time limit and reported against the Shipping Bill in EDPMS — add tracking of realisation per Shipping Bill.
- [ ] **(Legal)** Purpose codes and buyer KYC as required by the AD bank.

### 3.3 Paying Indian suppliers — decision needed — Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)
- [ ] **(Legal/Biz)** **Today supplier payouts go through Payoneer in USD.** A purchase from an Indian supplier by an Indian exporter is a domestic purchase, normally invoiced in INR with GST and paid from an Indian bank account. Decide the supplier payment route (INR bank transfer / payment gateway vs Payoneer) before setting Payoneer credentials.
- [ ] **(Legal/Biz)** **PO currency**: the PO is in USD while the supplier's GST invoice is in INR. Decide INR POs for Indian suppliers (with the FX risk on PortLess) or USD POs with an agreed conversion rule. The e-mails already show INR first with the USD contract price and rate (shared/currency.py); the PO document is still USD only.

### 3.4 Sanctions, KYC, AML — Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)
- [ ] **(Legal/Eng)** Counterparty screening uses the Apify `counterparty-check-ai` actor. Confirm it is an acceptable sanctions source for your bank and the escrow provider, or add a licensed screening provider; keep the evidence per deal.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Replace the DGFT stub (2.6).

### 3.5 Documents that do not exist yet — Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Phytosanitary certificate (plant quarantine) for grain and other agricultural exports — not generated or tracked today.
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Certificate of origin issued by the authorised body (the system issues an application, not the certificate) — track the issued certificate number.

### 3.6 Contracts and terms — Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)
- [ ] **(Legal)** Standard buyer terms (sales contract template), supplier purchase terms, and escrow terms — reviewed by counsel; quality claim procedure, force majeure, demurrage responsibility, governing law and arbitration.
- [ ] **(Legal)** Terms for the platform itself (privacy policy, terms of use) if buyers or suppliers sign in.

### 4 Money safety and financial controls
- [ ] **(Eng)** **No Mongo transactions anywhere.** A tranche release, a payout and the ledger / journal are written as separate documents. Wrap every money state change (escrow hold + tranche + ledger + deal status) in a transaction or an idempotent outbox, so a crash in the middle cannot leave money booked twice or not at all.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Daily reconciliation job: PortLess escrow ledger vs Tazapay / Escrow.com statement, payouts vs payout provider statement, bank receipts vs e-BRC. Any difference → `deal.exception` and the digest.
- [ ] **(Eng/QA)** Idempotency audit of every money endpoint and webhook (replay the same webhook 2×, 10× concurrently; release the same tranche from two operators at once). Catalogue case "staff and staff2 approve the gate at the same moment → exactly one call answers 200" is not automated yet.
- [ ] **(Biz)** Exposure limits: maximum value per deal, per buyer, per supplier, per day, and total money in escrow; refuse or escalate above them.
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** Roles: separate "can approve money" from "can operate deals"; today any allowlisted staff can approve any gate (four-eyes only above the threshold).

### 5.1 Found in the automation runs (open) — Workflow gaps found end to end
- [ ] **(Eng)** **PI acceptance does not check the price.** In UC-15 the LLM-played buyer wrote "We accept the Proforma Invoice … at USD 1,180" for a PI at USD 1,225; it was read as a plain acceptance and opened `CONFIRM_PI_ACCEPTANCE` with no flag. Compare any price in the acceptance with the PI and force review (`pi_price_mismatch`).

### 5.2 Steps that are still manual or stubbed — Workflow gaps found end to end
- [ ] **(Ops)** Customs filing (ICEGATE stub) — define the manual CHA process and the evidence recorded until the API works.

### 5.5 Add suppliers manually (local suppliers) — Workflow gaps found end to end
- [ ] **(Eng)** Supplier directory endpoints: `POST / PUT / DELETE /trade-min/suppliers` (staff). Fields: legal name, contact person, e-mail, phone / WhatsApp, full facility address with PIN code, GSTIN, IEC, Udyam, FSSAI / APEDA registration, products (HS codes, grades), capacity and MOQ in MT, usual price (INR or USD, FOB or ex-works), port of loading and distance to it, bank details for payment. Stored with `source="manual"`; a later scan never overwrites it; duplicates found by GSTIN, domain or phone.
- [ ] **(Eng)** On add or edit, run the same checks as a scanned supplier: GSTN / DGFT IEC / MCA / Udyam verification, sanctions screening and e-mail check; show the result on the record; a failed check flags the supplier and forces every gate that uses it to L0.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Start the workflow from a manual supplier**, three ways:
  - *Add to an existing prospect or deal*: the supplier joins the RFQ shortlist (RFQ e-mail with the `[RFQ:]` token through the `DISPATCH_RFQ` gate), or staff record the price heard by phone as its quote (`POST /sourcing/quotes`) — verification, match board and `CONFIRM_MATCH` follow as for any quote.
  - *Supply-first deal*: staff start a deal from a manual supplier and a product; PortLess runs the buyer search for the product's markets, the buyer outreach funnel, the match board and the negotiation as usual.
  - *Pre-arranged trade*: buyer and supplier both known and entered by staff — skip outreach and matching and open the negotiation (or record the agreed prices and go to the supplier's firm order request → PI).
- [ ] **(Eng)** RFQ leg: include the deal's supplier party, its supplier offers and directory suppliers linked to the deal in `_rfq_candidates`, so a hand-made deal never parks without an RFQ.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Suppliers page: "Add supplier" form, edit, verification badges, import CSV.
- [ ] **(Eng)** On a prospect / deal: "Add supplier to this deal" (pick from the directory or add new), "Record phone quote", and "Start a deal from this supplier".

### 6.1 Automation coverage — Quality assurance gaps
- [ ] **(QA/Eng)** Automate the 24 P0 catalogue cases that still have unautomated steps: TC-E2E-001 … TC-E2E-009 (written for the console), TC-EXC-005, TC-EXC-006, TC-FRT-017, TC-HIT-008, TC-HIT-017, TC-INT-012, TC-LED-006, TC-LED-008, TC-MAT-002, TC-MAT-014, TC-NEG-014, TC-NEG-017, TC-PAY-001, TC-PI-018, TC-REL-008.
- [ ] **(QA/Eng)** Automate the production-risk scenarios (`TEST_CASES.md` section 9, TC-RSK-001 … TC-RSK-095; 52 P0): money crashes and concurrency, escrow provider surprises, gates under pressure, ambiguous / foreign / automatic / hostile e-mails, supplier quotes in other units, compliance holds, inspection and carrier reality, restarts and outages, security, document consistency, currency and facility address, manual suppliers. Untagged cases must be confirmed on their first run; tag any that fail `@known-defect`.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Fix the defects the production-risk cases record (`TEST_CASES.md` section 10, DEF-12 … DEF-21) and decide DEC-08 … DEC-10 (overpayment, failed DUPRO, partial quality claims).

### 6.2 Browser (console) tests — Quality assurance gaps
- [ ] **(QA/Eng)** Browser automation as its own task in a **separate repository `portless-e2e` with Playwright** (chosen over Cypress: two operators in one test for four-eyes, several tabs for live updates and the Kill Switch, downloads, several origins, WebKit, free parallel runs). Plan, repository layout, sign-in stub and order of work: `docs/testing/BROWSER_SCENARIOS.md`.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Prerequisite for it: `data-testid` attributes on every console button and money figure (DEF-05).
- [ ] **(QA)** Automate the 17 P0 `@ui` cases and the money / security browser cases first (TC-WEB-001 … TC-WEB-008, TC-WEB-017, TC-WEB-019, TC-WEB-023, TC-WEB-025).

### 6.3 Real provider sandboxes — Quality assurance gaps
- [ ] **(QA)** Contract tests against each provider's own sandbox (Tazapay, QIMA ppapi, Maersk test environment, Escrow.com sandbox) — the in-house simulators follow public docs, which is exactly what is unconfirmed (section 2).

### 6.5 Security testing — Quality assurance gaps
- [ ] **(QA/Eng)** Penetration test before go-live: auth bypass, organisation isolation (one org reading another's deal), webhook forgery and replay, SSE token in query string, injection through e-mail bodies into LLM prompts.

### 6.7 Pilot — Quality assurance gaps
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops/QA)** One pilot trade end to end with real money in a small lot, known counterparties, every gate L0, a person watching each step; write down every manual intervention and fix it before the next deal.

### 6.8 Test infrastructure — Quality assurance gaps
- [ ] **(Eng)** CI for the backend: `pytest -q` (≈10 min) on every push and pull request, block merges to `main` on failure; re-measure coverage.
- [ ] **(Eng)** CI for the UI: `npm run lint` and `npm run build` on every push.

### 6.9 Unit tests — Quality assurance gaps
- [ ] **(Eng)** Keep the backend unit suite green on every change (4574 passed, 1 skipped on 2026-11-12) and re-measure coverage — 95.46 % line + branch was measured on 2026-11-09, before the latest features.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Coverage gate in CI: fail the build below 95 % overall and below 100 % branch coverage for the money modules (`escrow_guard` service / ledger / journal, `trade_costs`, `pricing`, `shared/currency.py`, `supplier_commitment`, `compliance`).

### 6.10 Integration tests — Quality assurance gaps
- [ ] **(Eng)** Integration tests against a real MongoDB (disposable container or a test Atlas cluster), not `mongomock`: transactions once added (DEF-13), unique indexes, concurrent updates of the same deal / escrow, `set_fields` atomicity, large documents.
- [ ] **(QA/Eng)** Provider contract tests against each provider's own sandbox (Tazapay, QIMA ppapi, Maersk test environment, Escrow.com sandbox) — see 6.3; run nightly and before every release.

### 6.11 Sanity and smoke checks — Quality assurance gaps
- [ ] **(Eng)** Post-deploy smoke test that blocks the rollout: `/health` of every service, `GET /health/readiness` all ok, database reachable, one read per service, SSE stream connects, an unsigned webhook is refused, the console loads and signs in.
- [ ] **(Eng/Ops)** Daily production sanity check (synthetic, alerting on failure): readiness, background watchers alive (heartbeat per watcher), event-bus consumer lag, mailbox send + receive, provider credentials still valid (QIMA token, Maersk OAuth, Tazapay key, Payoneer), Drive writable, LLM gateway answers.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Configuration sanity at boot: every required setting present, typed and not a `your_..._here` placeholder for the active profile (readiness covers most — add the missing ones, e.g. `FOUR_EYES_THRESHOLD_USD`, `PORTLESS_PUBLIC_BASE_URL`).

### 6.12 Regression suite — Quality assurance gaps
- [ ] **(QA/Eng)** Define the regression suite and when it runs — every pull request: backend unit + integration, UI lint + build + unit, catalogue `@P0 and not @known-defect`; every merge to `main`: + LLM journeys UC-01 and UC-15; nightly: whole catalogue, all 15 LLM journeys, browser `@ui`, provider sandboxes; before a release: everything plus the full browser matrix and a load run.
- [ ] **(QA)** Rule: every production incident and every bug fixed gets a regression test (unit or catalogue case) in the same change as the fix.

### 6.13 Static checks and test hygiene — Quality assurance gaps
- [ ] **(Eng)** Lint and type checks in CI: `ruff` (configured in pyproject, not enforced), `mypy` on `shared/` and the money services, `tsc --noEmit` for the UI.

### 7 Platform, infrastructure and operations
- [ ] **(Eng)** **The backend runs on Vercel serverless functions** (seen in the deployed logs: `/var/task`). A function is frozen once it answers and killed after its time limit, so nothing long-running works there: the automation view server and test runs (now refused with a clear 501), the deal watcher (reminders, stall alerts), the approval timeout watcher, the escrow / QC / freight pollers, the 15-minute IMAP poll, the daily digest, durable NATS consumers and the long-lived approvals stream (SSE). Move the backend to a long-running host (VM, Render, Railway, Fly, Docker) — or keep the API on Vercel and run the background jobs and automation on a separate long-running worker.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Durable event bus (NATS) — 1.5.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Background watchers have no lock.** The deal watcher, escrow watcher, HITL timeout watcher, QC / freight / escrow pollers and the digest run in every process. Two backend replicas would send every reminder twice and sweep twice. Add a leader lock (Mongo lease) or run them in one worker.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Backups: Mongo Atlas continuous backup with point-in-time restore; a restore drill; Google Drive document folder backed up.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Monitoring and alerting: error rate, webhook failures, stuck deals, open exceptions, money gate age, provider error rates, LLM failure rate; alerts to Telegram / e-mail; on-call owner.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Deployment: decide the monolith (`app.py`) vs per-service containers for production, health checks, rolling deploy, zero-downtime migrations.

### 8 Security and access
- [ ] **(Eng)** `PORTLESS_ACCOUNT_ID` set so other RouteToRich apps' tokens are refused.
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** Roles inside staff: trade desk, money approver, compliance officer, admin — today it is staff vs organisation user only.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Audit log that cannot be edited (approvals, kill switch, hold clearances, PI extensions, staff relays) — stored separately or hash-chained.

### 9 Operator console (portless-ui)
- [ ] **(Eng)** CI and tests (6.2, 6.8).

